<DocsLayout
  slug="dataset-versioning"
  title="Dataset Versioning with DVC | MIAR Documentation"
  description="How MIAR combines Git, DVC, and Cloudflare R2 to version imagery training corpora without storing large binaries in Git."
>
  <section id="purpose">
    <h2>What DVC does for MIAR</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://dvc.org/" rel="external">Data Version Control (DVC)</a> gives a large
      dataset a small, reviewable pointer that can live beside code in Git. The image and label
      binaries remain outside Git in object storage. The pointer records their content identity,
      so checking out a release commit and running <code>dvc pull</code> restores the corpus that
      commit expects.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <span>Plain language</span>
      <strong>Git answers which dataset version. DVC resolves its contents. R2 holds the bytes.</strong>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="relationship">
    <h2>Git, DVC, and R2 have different jobs</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Stores</th><th>Does not replace</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Git</td><td>Code, configuration, manifests, and small DVC pointer files</td><td>Large binary storage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DVC</td><td>Content-addressed identity and transfer instructions</td><td>Object storage or experiment tracking</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloudflare R2</td><td>Imagery, labels, derived corpus files, and immutable release binaries</td><td>Dataset lineage or release approval</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      MIAR uses R2 through its
      <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/get-started/s3/" rel="external">S3-compatible API</a>.
      DVC supports S3 remotes, allowing the corpus workflow to remain portable while storage stays
      inexpensive and independent from the training machine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="track">
    <h2>What belongs in a versioned corpus</h2>
    <ul class="docs-checklist">
      <li>Source scenes that are licensed and approved for the stated training purpose.</li>
      <li>Annotations, ontology version, and label-policy version used by the training job.</li>
      <li>Frozen train, validation, and test assignments with site-level leakage controls.</li>
      <li>Corpus manifest with checksums, source lineage, sensor, GSD, dimensions, and quality fields.</li>
      <li>Derived chips or preprocessed tensors when the model consumes them directly.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Credentials, arbitrary local caches, unreviewed machine suggestions, and redistributable
      imagery without a valid license do not belong in the tracked corpus.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="workflow">
    <h2>Register and publish a corpus revision</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Curate</strong><p>Validate rights, metadata, annotations, split integrity, and checksums before registration.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Track</strong><p>Use DVC to create a content-addressed pointer for the reviewed corpus directory.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>Publish</strong><p>Push binaries to the configured R2 remote, then commit the pointer and manifest to Git.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Resolve</strong><p>Training checks out the intended Git revision and pulls the exact DVC content before execution.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>dvc add path/to/corpus
git add path/to/corpus.dvc path/to/.gitignore
dvc push
git commit -m "Register governed corpus revision"</code></pre>
    <p>To reconstruct a release candidate:</p>
    <pre class="docs-code"><code>git checkout &lt;release-commit&gt;
dvc pull</code></pre>
  </section>

